            <fr:title text="List Decoding and Proximity Gaps">List Decoding and Proximity Gaps</fr:title>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>
    The technical core of every soundness proof in the <fr:link href="/dhsorens-0037/" title="FRI" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0037/" display-uri="dhsorens-0037" type="local">FRI</fr:link> family, and the place where deployed parameters stop being provably sound and start being conjecturally sound. It is the most load-bearing page in the section.
</html:p>
            <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>The three regimes — unique decoding, the Johnson bound, capacity — and what parameters each licenses.</html:li>
        <html:li>What a proximity gap statement says, and why a low-degree test cannot be analysed without one.</html:li>
        <html:li>Correlated agreement and mutual correlated agreement; which protocols need the stronger form.</html:li>
        <html:li>The conjectures: which are theorems, which are conjectures, and which deployed systems assume which. The SoK argues these regimes are routinely conflated in the primary sources.</html:li>
        <html:li>Machine-checked threshold constants, and whether the formalisation can be reused in <fr:link href="/dhsorens-0040/" title="ArkLib" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0040/" display-uri="dhsorens-0040" type="local">ArkLib</fr:link>.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
            <html:p>
    Sources: <fr:link href="https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/2046" type="external">On Reed–Solomon Proximity Gaps Conjectures</fr:link>, <fr:link href="https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/858" type="external">FRI Soundness Above the Johnson Bound via Threshold Halving</fr:link>, <fr:link href="https://deltastar.computer/" type="external">Pinning <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\delta ^*]]></fr:tex></fr:link>, <fr:link href="https://hackmd.io/@zkpunk/CS25" type="external">notes on the proximity-gap conjectures</fr:link>.
</html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
